From fccf2d1f19f044cd3519066cbf355af105fcbaf1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 00:10:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] perf(parser): gate the autolink pre-flight on a document
 scan
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every block of a GFM document ran `may_contain_autolink` over its inline
content: a `memchr2` pass for `@` and `:`, plus the `www.` substring
search whenever no `@` turned up. On prose without URLs each of those
passes reads the paragraph again after the block and inline marker scans
and finds nothing, once per block.

The root parser now scans its normalized source for the same three
needles once, next to the definition pre-pass, and shares the verdict
with sub-parsers like the other pre-pass results. When the document
holds no `@`, no `www.`, and no `://`, no block of it can hold an
autolink candidate and `parse_inline_block` skips the pre-flight
outright; otherwise the per-block check runs exactly as before.

The verdict is a superset of every block verdict: a sub-source is its
parent's own bytes, copied whole lines at a time, with only spaces, line
endings, and unescaped table pipes written between the copied runs, none
of which occur in a needle, so no needle can appear in a sub-source
without appearing in the root source. The document scan deliberately
keeps the `](http://` destinations that the block check excludes, which
only widens it further. Documents with autolinks off never scan at all,
and the block-only definition phase builds no inline content.

The differential tests parse both spec fixtures — example by example and
whole — plus generated documents that put every needle shape into every
container, under four option profiles, and compare full AST `Debug`
output against a parser forced back to the ungated state.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
