From 06513b69a8c5a15e65193b69199e2e05adf99e3e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 00:14:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] perf(parser): narrow the block autolink pre-flight to the
 document needles
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

When the document gate is open, the per-block pre-flight still ran both
of its searches on every block, including the ones looking for a needle
the document does not contain anywhere.

`may_contain_autolink` now takes the document verdict and drops the
search whose needle the root scan ruled out: no `www.` in the source
means the substring search can only come back empty, so `may_have_www`
is just the block's own `@`; no `@` and no `://` in the source means the
`memchr2` pass can raise neither `has_at` nor `has_bare_scheme`, and
`may_have_extended` needs an `@` of its own, so the whole pass goes. A
document that holds every needle runs exactly what it ran before.

The block content is a copy of document bytes, so a needle in the block
is a needle in the document and every skipped search is one that was
going to fail. The pre-flight tests now assert that directly: for each
case, every document verdict that covers the content's own needles —
which is every verdict the root scan can produce for a source holding
it — must give the same result as the unrestricted one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
